A||N||8||Seanh||Puking Fiero. Help!||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||05-10-2001||09:34 PM||||Ok, about 2 weeks ago my car did this the first time. It puked coolant, out the top of the overflow tank, all over the floor after I came home after a night of driving. I didn't get to concerned, just filled up the tank and drove off. It pulled this crap again the other day. I was driving and noticed it overheating. I pulled over the it was puking up coolant, again from the top of the overflow tank. I again filled it and drove off. Now today, it did it again. I had been driving for about 2 hours around town with friends. I parked the car for about 10 minuts, came out and started it up, and just backed up to talk to another person and I started to pull away and noticed the huge puddle of coolant under the car and it was overheating again. I stopped, let it cool down for an hour or so. Came back and filled the tank up and it sucked quite a bit down and I filled it up and let it idle for about 20 minuts while watching it and keeping it filled. It got to a point where it didn't take any more, so I put the cap on the tank and drove off and cruised around a while with no further trouble. I went home and parked it for about another hour, came back out and noticed coolant on the floor. I didn't do anything, and just drove off again, with no further overheating problems. I just got home, and haven't checked on it again yet, so I don't know if its going to hold this one down or not.<P>So what the heck could be causeing this? Is the engine getting to hot and boiling the coolant or what? <P>BTW, I should let you guys know this probally, I use very little anti-freeze during summer, I use mostly water in the cooling system as I have been told it dissipates heat better with more water. Don't know if this is true, but I do notice it running a little cooler with mostly water. The car has done this puking act with anti-freeze also, so I don't think its related but I just didn't want any questionables in there.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mark||05-10-2001||09:56 PM||||Could be a bad radiator cap.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||05-10-2001||10:06 PM||||I 2nd the rad cap motion if the temp gauge isn't climbing excessively.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||05-10-2001||10:07 PM||||Hmmm. Somthing to consider indeed at this point. I just went out and checked, and it did it again!<P>Coolant has spewed out of the top of the overflow tank, and the overflow tank is now empty and the system would be sucking in air if I started it now.||||reg||1
Z||000004||RNGCRSH||05-10-2001||10:10 PM||||you need to run anti-freeze it also raises the boiling point of water when at a proper ratio. the reason it is puking is because of gas pressure in you radiator is exceeding the presure rating of you cap.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||05-10-2001||11:04 PM||||Ok, one thing though. If it was the cap, wouldn't it do it while driving down the road also? It doesn't. It only does this after I park the car for a few minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000006||84Bill||05-11-2001||01:03 PM||||welp you dont want to fill the tank to the top because its gonna barf it out when hot.<BR>What ya need to do is, when cool fill it from the rear then fill the front (CAP not the tank). run it until it is warmed up and let it cool then do the process over again.<P>The car will run up to almost the 3rd tick on the temp gage and the fan should kick on to cool things off, it will most likely only go down to just a smidge below 1/2 before it shuts off.<P>Oh and its a good idea to replace both front and rear caps just to be sure.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 05-11-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000007||Raydar||05-11-2001||01:16 PM||||Make sure that the cap is the UNvented type. Apparently, STANT has the incorrect part number on the cap they sell for a Fiero. If you just go by the chart and the part number, you've probably got the wrong cap. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||05-11-2001||07:06 PM||||Why, whats wrong with the vented version? I got into an argument with NAPA today about it. I said I needed the sealed one, they told me all GM cars and trucks of that year only used vented, and if I were using a sealed one, chances are that it what is causing my problem. I did go ahead and buy the vented one because I can't be without a car Friday night of course. I poped it on, filled it up, and she's running good again. No puking problems, and it seems like better pressure in the hoses then before. How are should the hose's be while its running warmed up? Mine get alot stiffer now then before the new cap. I probally had a problem with it for a while. Thanks||||reg||1
